"CSS Snapshot 2026 (https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-css-2026-20260622/)\nciteturn1319view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.w3.org/TR/2026/NOTE-css-2026-20260622/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 4966\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Cascading and Inheritance Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-5/)\nciteturn1319view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1315view1\",\"pattern\":\"4. Value Processing\"}); Total lines: 1419\nL49:   1. cite28†1 Introduction L50:     1. cite29†1.1 Module Interactions L51:   2. cite30†2 Importing Style Sheets: the @import rule L52:     1. cite31†2.1 Conditional @import Rules L53:     2. cite32†2.2 Processing Stylesheet Imports L54:     3. cite33†2.3 Content-Type of CSS Style Sheets L55:   3. cite34†3 Shorthand Properties L56:     1. cite35†3.1 Property Aliasing L57:     2. cite36†3.2 Resetting All Properties: the all property L58:   4. cite37†4 Value Processing L59:     1. cite38†4.1 Declared Values L60:       1. cite39†4.1.1 Value Aliasing L61:     2. cite40†4.2 Cascaded Values L269: ## 4. Value Processing\nL270: \nL271: Once a user agent has parsed a document and constructed a document tree, it must assign, to every element in the cite199†flat tree , and correspondingly to every box in the formatting structure, a value to every property that applies to the target cite200†media type .\nL272: \nL273: The final value of a CSS property for a given element or box is the result of a multi-step calculation:\nL349: Certain CSS features can interfere with value processing on a per-fragment basis. See for example cite246†CSS Pseudo-Elements 4 § 2.1.3 Inheritance and the ::first-line Pseudo-element which alters inheritance for fragments within the cite247†::first-line pseudo-element.\nL350: In such cases, where individual fragments are given different cite115†specified values , any values that resolve based on the cite203†computed value of other properties (such as cite248†currentcolor or cite212†em units) are resolved per cite249†box fragment . Subsequent value processing proceeds as normal in each fragment.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Text Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-text-3/)\nciteturn1319view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1316view1\",\"pattern\":\"exact rules\"}); Total lines: 4797\nL2454: Hyphenation occurs when the line breaks at a valid hyphenation opportunity, which is a type of cite906†soft wrap opportunity that exists within a word where cite4087†hyphenation is allowed. In CSS cite4088†hyphenation opportunities are controlled with the cite3349†hyphens property. CSS Text Level 3 does not define the exact rules for hyphenation; however UAs are strongly encouraged to optimize their choice of break points and to chose language-appropriate hyphenation points.\nL2455: \nL2456: Tests\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Conditional Rules Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-conditional-5/)\nciteturn1319view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1316view3\",\"pattern\":\"computed value of a property on the query container\"}); Total lines: 1578\nL400: The 40em value used in the query condition is relative to the cite187†computed value†www.w3.org of cite185†font-size†www.w3.org on the relevant cite145†query container .\nL401: \nL402: Containers can also expose computed style values for querying. This can be useful for toggling behavior across multiple properties:\nL403: \nL404:     @container style(--cards: small) {\nL405:       article {\nL406:       border: thin solid silver;\nL407:       border-radius: 0.5em;\nL408:       padding: 1em;\nL409:       }\nL410:     }\nL639: The 40em value used in the query condition is relative to the cite187†computed value†www.w3.org of cite185†font-size†www.w3.org on the relevant cite145†query container :\nL640: \nL641:   * For any h2 inside aside, the query condition will be true above 640px.\nL642: \nL643:   * For any h2 inside main, the query condition will be true above 960px.\nL644: Similarly, cite145†query containers will evaluate cite243†var()†www.w3.org -based queries relative to their own cite187†computed value†www.w3.org of the cite240†custom property†www.w3.org :\nL645: \nL646:     aside, main {\nL647:       container-type: inline-size;\nL648:     }\nL649: \nL650:     aside { --query: 300px; }\nL651:     main { --query: 500px; }\nL652: \nL653:     @container (width > var(--query)) {\nL654:       h2 { font-size: 1.5em; }\nL655:     }\nL656: The var(--query) value used in the query condition is substituted with the cite187†computed value†www.w3.org of the --query cite240†custom property†www.w3.org on the relevant cite145†query container :\nL657: \nL658:   * For any h2 inside aside, the query condition will be true above 300px.\nL659: \nL660:   * For any h2 inside main, the query condition will be true above 500px.\nL661: #### 6.1.1. Width: the cite244†width feature\nL662: \nL663: Name:  | width\nL664: For:  | cite152†@container L665: Value:  | cite245†<length>†www.w3.org L666: Type:  | range\nL667: \nL668: The cite244†width cite223†container feature queries the cite246†width†www.w3.org of the cite145†query container ’s cite247†content box†www.w3.org .\nL669: #### 6.1.2. Height: the cite248†height feature\nL670: \nL671: Name:  | height\nL672: For:  | cite152†@container L673: Value:  | cite245†<length>†www.w3.org L674: Type:  | range\nL675: \nL676: The cite248†height cite223†container feature queries the cite249†height†www.w3.org of the cite145†query container ’s cite247†content box†www.w3.org .\nL677: #### 6.1.3. Inline-size: the cite226†inline-size feature\nL678: \nL679: Name:  | inline-size\nL680: For:  | cite152†@container L681: Value:  | cite245†<length>†www.w3.org L682: Type:  | range\nL683: \nL684: The cite226†inline-size cite223†container feature queries the cite250†size†www.w3.org of the cite145†query container ’s cite247†content box†www.w3.org in the query container’s cite177†inline axis†www.w3.org .\nL685: #### 6.1.4. Block-size: the cite251†block-size feature\nL686: \nL687: Name:  | block-size\nL688: For:  | cite152†@container L689: Value:  | cite245†<length>†www.w3.org L690: Type:  | range\nL691: \nL692: The cite251†block-size cite223†container feature queries the cite250†size†www.w3.org of the cite145†query container ’s cite247†content box†www.w3.org in the query container’s cite178†block axis†www.w3.org .\nL693: #### 6.1.5. Aspect-ratio: the cite252†aspect-ratio feature\nL694: \nL695: Name:  | aspect-ratio\nL696: For:  | cite152†@container L697: Value:  | cite253†<ratio>†www.w3.org L698: Type:  | range\nL699: \nL700: The cite252†aspect-ratio cite223†container feature is defined as the ratio of the value of the cite244†width container feature to the value of the cite248†height container feature.\nL701: #### 6.1.6. Orientation: the cite254†orientation feature\nL702: \nL703: Name:  | orientation\nL704: For:  | cite152†@container L705: Value:  | portrait cite101†|†www.w3.org landscape\nL706: Type:  | discrete\nL707: \nL708: portrait\nL709:     The cite254†orientation cite223†container feature is cite255†portrait when the value of the cite248†height container feature is greater than or equal to the value of the cite244†width container feature.\nL710: landscape\nL713: A container style query allows querying the cite187†computed values†www.w3.org of the cite145†query container . It is a boolean combination of individual style features (cite210†<style-feature> ) that each query a single, specific property of the query container. The syntax of a <style-feature> is either the same as for a valid cite257†declaration†www.w3.org cite258†[CSS-SYNTAX-3] , a cite214†<style-feature-name> or a valid style range (cite213†<style-range> ).\nL715: A cite211†<style-feature-plain> evaluates to true if the cite187†computed value†www.w3.org of the given property on the cite145†query container matches the given value (which is also computed with respect to the query container), and false otherwise.\nL716: \nL717: A cite262†style feature without a value (cite212†<style-feature-boolean> ) evaluates to true if the cite187†computed value†www.w3.org is different from the cite196†initial value†www.w3.org for the given cite263†property†www.w3.org .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Box Sizing Module Level 3 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-sizing-3/)\nciteturn1319view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1315view2\",\"pattern\":\"5.2.1. Cyclic Percentages\"}); Total lines: 1331\nL62:     3. cite41†3.3 Box Edges for Sizing: the box-sizing property L63:     4. cite42†3.4 New Column Sizing Values: the stretch, min-content, max-content, and fit-content values L64:   4. cite43†4 Extrinsic Size Determination L65:     1. cite44†4.1 Percentage Sizing L66:     2. cite45†4.2 Stretch-fit Sizing: filling the containing block L67:   5. cite46†5 Intrinsic Size Determination L68:     1. cite47†5.1 Intrinsic Sizes L69:     2. cite48†5.2 Intrinsic Contributions L70:       1. cite49†5.2.1 Intrinsic Contributions of Percentage-Sized Boxes L1027:   * cite656†border-box , in § 3.3\nL1028:   * cite199†<box-size> , in § 3.2\nL1029:   * cite115†box-sizing , in § 3.3\nL1030:   * cite1119†content-box , in § 3.3\nL1031:  